WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.760
A rules file is plain Markdown the agent reads at the start of every session.

00:00:05.110 --> 00:00:07.429
Claude Code calls it CLAUDE.md.

00:00:07.429 --> 00:00:10.870
Codex and many other agents call it AGENTS.md.

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:13.319
Put in it what you'd tell a new teammate:

00:00:13.319 --> 00:00:16.852
build commands, conventions, where things live, and what never to do.

00:00:18.252 --> 00:00:20.492
Rules load from broad to specific.

00:00:20.842 --> 00:00:24.048
Your organization's file, then yours, then the project's,

00:00:24.048 --> 00:00:27.029
then any folder below it, once the agent works there.

00:00:27.379 --> 00:00:31.027
They're combined, not replaced, and the closest one is read last.

00:00:31.377 --> 00:00:33.632
All of it is sent with every request,

00:00:33.632 --> 00:00:36.070
so every line costs context, every time.

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:38.793
Here's a short one.

00:00:39.143 --> 00:00:43.388
It says: use pnpm, not npm, and run the tests before committing.

00:00:43.738 --> 00:00:46.063
An @ import pulls in another file,

00:00:46.063 --> 00:00:49.413
like the AGENTS.md your other tools already read.

00:00:49.763 --> 00:00:52.987
And a rule in .claude/rules can carry a paths field,

00:00:52.987 --> 00:00:56.397
so it loads only when the agent touches matching files.

00:00:57.797 --> 00:00:59.678
Keep it under two hundred lines.

00:00:59.678 --> 00:01:02.853
Longer files cost more context, and get followed less.

00:01:03.203 --> 00:01:07.577
Imports organize a file, but they don't shrink it: imported files load too.

00:01:07.927 --> 00:01:11.936
If two rules contradict each other, the agent may pick one arbitrarily,

00:01:11.936 --> 00:01:12.727
so prune them.

00:01:13.077 --> 00:01:14.567
Codex reads AGENTS.md,

00:01:14.567 --> 00:01:18.495
and stops adding files at thirty-two kilobytes by default.
